panvk: run on Arm's mali_kbase through the kbase pan_kmod backend

- Enumeration: kbase's /dev/mali0 is not a DRM device, so drmGetDevices2() never lists it. PanVK
  gets an `enumerate` callback (as Turnip has for KGSL, src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_knl.cc) that opens
  the kbase node (PANVK_KBASE_DEVICE, default /dev/mali0) and creates the physical device without a
  drmDevice; when the node is absent or the kbase backend refuses it, VK_ERROR_INCOMPATIBLE_DRIVER
  lets the runtime enumerate DRM devices (src/vulkan/runtime/vk_instance.c). Without a drmDevice
  there are no DRM node ids and no VK_EXT_physical_device_drm.
- The CSF queue code issues its panthor ioctls and reads the CSF interface and flush id through
  pan_kmod_dev_ioctl(), pan_kmod_dev_get_csif_props() and pan_kmod_dev_get_flush_id() (same
  behaviour on panthor).
- VA heaps: the executable heap is the first 4 GiB window of the backend's user VA range instead of
  [32 MiB, 4 GiB) (unchanged for a range starting at 0); the capture/replay heap stays above it.
- Private BOs and the render descriptor ring are GPU-mapped before they are CPU-mapped: a backend
  may create the memory only when it gets its GPU address.
- On a backend that maps a BO once (props.limits.single_gpu_mapping), the render descriptor ring
  is one BO of twice the size instead of one BO mapped twice; sparse binding is off.
- No VK_EXT_map_memory_placed without placed CPU mappings, no external-fd extensions without fd
  sharing.
- Sync objects command streams wait on live in GPU_SYNC memory when the backend offers it: the
  uncached pool (VkEvents, queries) carries the flag, and the queues' own sync objects and the
  render descriptor ring's come from that pool instead of the cached one. A BO with a fixed_va is
  mapped there instead of at an address from the VA heaps.
- vkSetEvent tells the kernel the CPU changed a sync object (pan_kmod_dev_notify_sync_update), so
  a group suspended on the event's wait runs again.

